A||||5||sid1200||emissions
Z||000000||sid1200||06-30-99||02:47 AM||||does anyone know if all fiero's were made to CA spec emissions standards, most low volume cars would be.  need help, thinking of brining an out of state one into ca<br>thanks
Z||000001||theogre||06-30-99||07:34 AM||||Don't count on it. Fiero's collection of parts from other cars makes it very easy to build federal and cal setups as needed. It's just a matter of sticking a few diferant parts into the line.<p>I have noticed that there are not many things listed as diferant in the parts books for a CA car, but I haven't really had reson to check the whole catalog for such things.<p>
Z||000002||sid1200||06-30-99||11:20 AM||||yea I figured it that, but then I checked the Fiero store catalog, and they don't have any diff, parts of CA, even allt the emissions control stuff is the same apparently, unless I missed something. This does seem weird in relation to previous reply.  if anyone has more info, or who I could talk to I would appreciate it.
Z||000003||theogre||06-30-99||06:50 PM||||I found it.....  <A HREF="http://www.cruzers.com/~ludis/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cruzers.com/~ludis/</A>  has a list of Fiero ECM proms. It's the prom that appears to be the only differance.<p>That being the case, you could likely get the prom from a wreck of the same year or try ordering one from GM if the car doesn't pass the test first try. 84-86 4 cyl proms interchange, and 87-88 4cyl proms can be swapped. You CANNOT swap proms across the 86-87 modle year boundry for 4cyls'. The ECM's where completely changed for 87 on. I don't think it's a problem with V6 since they are all distributor motors, but I can't tell you for sure. look thru the table at the web site above.<p>Fiero motors that are in good order generally are pretty clean. I was suprised how low my numbers where when I took the thing thru DE inspection in may. One number allowed 400, mine was 200. the other 2 numbers where almost zilch. This is a car with over 100K miles on it.<p>One thing you could do before you buy it maybe is take the CA numbers with you and get someone to test it for you. If it's at least close the CA prom should help. If it's way off then the prom might not be enough. Proms are good for small changes to things. the big ones take work.<p>I would still try calling DMV. They have a bunch of rules about bringing in a car. I do believe it's avalable in print. Try searchin from the state web site  <A HREF="http://www.state.ca.us" TARGET=_blank>http://www.state.ca.us</A> 
Z||000004||terryk||08-08-99||01:02 PM||||Only the chips are different. The engine is the same. The Federal will pass CA emissions.<p>I haven't compared the differences between the chips, but it is possible they are the same. For some odd reason, GM will change the ID, but not the code for CA to Federal.<p>In the Buick 3.8L SC's there are only 4 byte different and none are emissions related (shift).<p>Terry
Z||000005||terryk||08-08-99||01:06 PM||||Bringing a 49 state car into California is easy. Depending on the state, many mandate CA emissions package (Nevada, Ohio?, some others). Even with out it, you pay a flat fee and go in with life. Very simple.<p>There is no reason a good running Fiero wouldn't pass. Keep in mind that some area's of CA are treadmilling. The pass/fail limits are very wide (to provide a false sense of security). When they tighten up over the next few years, even good running cars could fail. But a new cat will always do wonders.
